  "description": "Evaluate feedforward pass through 2-layer network with hidden ReLU activation and linear output.\n\nFeedforward neural networks with a single non-linear hidden layer can approximate any continuous function on compact subsets of R^n to arbitrary precision.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{X} \\mathbf{W}_1 + \\mathbf{b}_1) \\mathbf{W}_2 + \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute affine transformation X W1 + b1\n2. Apply element-wise rectified linear unit (ReLU) activation\n3. Project through second linear transformation W2 + b2 and round output to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list representing network predictions.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Multi-Layer Perceptron (Forward Pass)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **Multi-Layer Perceptron (Forward Pass)** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nFeedforward neural networks with a single non-linear hidden layer can approximate any continuous function on compact subsets of R^n to arbitrary precision. In this problem, the goal is to: evaluate feedforward pass through 2-layer network with hidden relu activation and linear output.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{X} \\mathbf{W}_1 + \\mathbf{b}_1) \\mathbf{W}_2 + \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute affine transformation X W1 + b1\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply element-wise rectified linear unit (ReLU) activation\n3. **Phase 3**: Project through second linear transformation W2 + b2 and round output to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X = [[1.0, 2.0], [-1.0, 0.5]], W1 = [[0.5, -0.5], [1.0, 0.0]], b1 = [0.1, -0.1], W2 = [[1.0], [-1.0]], b2 = [0.0]\n\nOutput: [[2.6], [-0.3]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated dl_forward_pass under standard test conditions."
